an><span class="enc-snip">Dadabhai Naoroji was an Indian political leader, economist and scholar from a Parsi family of Bombay.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dadda-ii"><span class="enc-name">Dadda II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dadda II was a king of the Gurjara dynasty of Nandipuri, in southern Gujarat, in the first half of the 7th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dadu"><span class="enc-name">Dadu</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dadu, also called Khanbaliq, was the capital of the Yuan Dynasty, built by Kublai Khan on the site of present-day Beijing.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dadusha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/dadusha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dadusha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dadusha was a king of Eshnunna in the Diyala region of Mesopotamia in the early 18th century BCE, the brother of Naram-Sin and son of Ipiq-Adad II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dae-gwang-hyeon"><span class="enc-name">Dae Gwang-hyeon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dae Gwang-hyeon was the crown prince of Balhae, a descendant of its founder Dae Joyeong, who led a large body of refugees to Goryeo after the Khitan Liao dynasty…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dae-ijin"><span class="enc-name">Dae Ijin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dae Ijin was the eleventh king of Balhae, reigning from 830 to 857, and grandson of King Seon, under whom the kingdom had reached its greatest extent.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dae-inseon"><span class="enc-name">Dae Inseon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dae Inseon was the fifteenth and last king of Balhae,